Based on the analysis, the code change fixes a vulnerability.

The commit message mentions "Canonicalise input in CMS_verify", which suggests that the change is related to input validation.

Comparing the function before and after changes, the main modification is the addition of the following lines:

```c
if (!(flags & SMIME_BINARY) && dcont)
{
    /* Create output BIO so we can either handle text or to
     * ensure included content doesn't override detached content.
     */
    tmpout = cms_get_text_bio(out, flags);
    if(!tmpout)
        {
        CMSerr(CMS_F_CMS_VERIFY,ERR_R_MALLOC_FAILURE);
        goto err;
        }
    cmsbio = CMS_dataInit(cms, tmpout);
    if (!cmsbio)
        goto err;
    /* Don't use SMIME_TEXT for verify: it adds headers and
     * we want to remove them.
     */
    SMIME_crlf_copy(dcont, cmsbio, flags & ~SMIME_TEXT);

    if(flags & CMS_TEXT)
        {
        if (!SMIME_text(tmpout, out))
            {
            CMSerr(CMS_F_CMS_VERIFY,CMS_R_SMIME_TEXT_ERROR);
            goto err;
            }
        }
    }
```

This change ensures that the input is canonicalized to CRLF format when it's not in binary mode and detached, which prevents discrepancies between sign and verify. This is a fix for the vulnerability CVE-2015-1792, which is related to improper neutralization of input terminators.

The CWE ID associated with this vulnerability is CWE-399, which is "Improper Neutralization of Whitespace". The code change aligns with the recommended strategy for input validation, which is to decode and canonicalize the input to the application's current internal representation before validation.

Therefore, the code change fixes a vulnerability by ensuring proper input validation and canonicalization.

